In article <1993Jul1.130823.15308@umiami.ir.miami.edu>
 misc.legal alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk writes:
>
>  (quoting Phill Hallam-Baker)
>=
>=The number of these threats and the abusiveness of his own postings suggests
>=that Dr Godfrey may be engaged in a deliberate strategy of inviting attacks
>=such that he may later sue for libel. Such a scheme is a criminal offence in
>=both the UK and the US, in the latter it has the status of a federal offence.

As I mentioned a while back on misc.legal, when I wrote for _The
Tech,_ MIT's mainstream student paper, I studied the _Associated Press
Stylebook and Libel Manual_.  There was nothing in the libel manual
about "incitement to libel".  It seems like a nonsensical concept to
me (even more nonsensical than real legal concepts :-).  I've heard
of incitement to *criminal* acts against third parties, but to incite
someone else to commit a *civil* offense against the inciter ... naah.

Please cite the section of the US Code that makes incitement to libel
illegal.

>=...complaints concerning Godfrey's
>=posts that arrived at DESY numbered over 100. These were sent to the
>=directorate, the heads of H1, ZEUS and almost every system manager. These
>=divide into complaints that his posts are merely offensive, those that
>=consider them racist and those that consider him an anti-semite....
>=
>= Making inflamatory statements with the
>=intention of profiting from libel suits arising from the equally inflamatory
>=replies is not only unethical it is illegal.

The First Amendment gives one a broad freedom to make inflammatory
statements, including racist and anti-semitic ones, over a common
carrier (e.g., the phone lines).  If Godfrey actually encourages
people to beat up Jews, say, *and* some netnews readers actually went
out and busted heads, the victims might be able to sue Godfrey.

>=This message has been written with the purpose of informing the USEnet
>=community of which CERN is a part, of a person engaging in abuse of
>=the network. It is thus excluded from any libel proceedings on the grounds
>=of diplomatic immunity (amongst others).

"Diplomatic immunity" has a precise definition, laid down by treaty.
If Mr. Hallam-Baker, as a representative of the Swiss Government has
presented his diplomatic credentials to the proper authorities in the
US State Department, and the State Department has accepted him into
this country as a diplomat (not merely as the representative of an
international organization, or even as a staff member of an embassy),
then yes, he would be covered by diplomatic immunity.  However, if
he were actually sued or charged with a crime, the Swiss Government
could choose to waive his diplomatic immunity -- the immunity is a
right which belongs to the government, not its representative.

(Source: _A Modern Introduction to International Law,_ which is not so
modern anymore -- it doesn't mention _Nicaragua v. US_ -- but I think
it'll do.)

