No, I agree that the policies SHOULD be black and white, but they sometimes are not.
However, just because you CAN access the data doesn't mean you should.  That is my
point.  I don't claim that the system doesn't have the responsibility to make it
clear what is and is not acceptable, it does.  I, however, claim that the user has
the responsibility to use common sense.

In this case, the person doesn't claim ignorance and the difference is one of
policy.  According to what they told him, the 'acceptable use' is to access ONLY
files that are 'his.'  If this policy is not written, explained or burned into
the computer screen, then it is not reasonable.  Curiosity is natural.  It is not
presumed that you can't access 'anything outside of your files.'

Now, in this case, the user did consider the implications, decided that there was
a legit reason to access the file and that there was no security violation.  As
I said in my earlier posts, the IS acceptable if it isn't against written policy.

I don't claim that the user should determine what is and is not acceptable for
every occasion.  The sysadmin has the responsibility to point out what is and is
not acceptable.  However, just because you are capable of accessing a peice of
information, DOESN'T give you the right.  If you do access information you
shouldn't, but there is NO WAY you can know that you shouldn't, that is ignorance
of the 'hidden policy' and thereby an excuse.  Accessing data you KNOW you
shouldn't, however, no matter what the reason is NOT acceptable.

My testing of 'novice' users was to test the 'warning level' of the name and
