Perhaps you are thinking of a BBS (inferred from your signature) which
provides E-mail capability, where reading of others' E-mail by the
sysop may be common.  The incident in question may have occurred in a
different environment.  I am guessing that the student had their
private E-mail read from their account on a Unix machine or network.
If so, allow me to describe the environment (for the benefit of people
not familiar with Unix)...

In a Unix environment, E-mail capability is a basic service, and
user-based access control to files and services is provided.
Typically, the operating system would not permit a professor to read
the E-mail of another user.

If the system is set up properly, only the "root" user can override the
protection on the E-mail and read it.  The "root" account is used for
system maintenance activities, and the password to it should only be
known to few people.  Unless this system is maintained by the
professor, the professor should not have "root" access.  (Could the
original poster please describe *how* the professor was able to read
private E-mail?)  In any case, "root" access is not intended for spying
on users of the system.

In absence of policy stating otherwise, there is generally an
expectation among users of a Unix system that E-mail to their personal
account is private and will not be viewed or tampered with by others
(including professors, managers, and system administration staff).

Similarly, it is generally expected that E-mail sent from their
personally account will not be intercepted before it reaches the
intended recipient(s).

(The above is my impression based on experience in commercial and
academic environments, as a user and system administrator.  I welcome
informed corrections or other viewpoints.)
