From caf-talk Caf Jul  6 14:55:10 1993
From: bachelu@mercury.cs.uregina.ca (Blaine Bachelu)
Subject:  Network Monitoring
Message-ID: <1993Jul06.164623.412008@sue.cc.uregina.ca>
Date: Tue, 06 Jul 1993 16:46:23 GMT

I am a Graduate Student at the University of Regina, Department
of Computer Science, and my research deals with privacy and 
security in a network environment.  
	
A little background information will probably help right about now.
My motivation for this research originates from the ready 
availability of public domain software which allows any computer 
to read details of all transmission packets on the network.
The purpose of my research is to investigate expectations of privacy, 
and to document by actually monitoring volunteers the dangers of 
maintenance of privacy.  In addition I will identify exactly what 
types of information can be captured through monitoring of transmission
packets in order to develop practices to protect transmission privacy.

Now, you are probably asking "What does this have to do with me?"
To round out my thesis, I would like to include some of the legal
aspects which arise when dealing with network monitoring.  That is
monitoring of electronic communications as they are transmitted 
over a communication medium.  And more specifically, the ability 
to read the contents of other peoples communications. What I am
looking for are any precedents or statutes (I'm in Canada, dealing with
Canadian laws, but I would also appreciate hearing about other countries
laws) which deal with electronic mail or electronic communication over 
a computer network.  I know there are a number of vague comparisons 
within the Canadian Criminal Code dealing with the equivalent of wire-tapping 
but I was wondering if there were some recent precedents.


Not being well versed in law I have come to a couple of conclusions
which may be somewhat naive. I would appreciate any corrections or
feedback which you may offer.

  - My most idealistic view of privacy laws comes down to the 
    expectation of privacy.  If there is a common level of expected
    privacy dealing with electronic communications then to make 
    an attempt to monitor the individual's electronic communications
    may be an offense.  Now, if there are implementations to increase
    the security and privacy of the communication then the expectation
    of privacy increases.  However, if it is known that security and
    privacy is minimal to non-existent can there be any recourse?
    I think not. How can there be an invasion of privacy if your 
    communications are public.  The TCP/IP protocol uses a broadcast
    method of communication, sending a message to each and every 
    computer on the network, and having every computer reading each
    and every packet on the network.  This is similar to passing 
    around a postcard to every person in your building, and the 
    person to which it is addressed making a photocopy, and then 
    passing the card along to the nest person.  Obviously, this is
    a public communication.

  - Is there any reasoning behind a company monitoring its employees
    electronic communications if those communications take place over
    the company's network (communication medium)?  Would the legality
    of the monitoring be different if the employees were informed of
    a company policy of monitoring communications on the company
    network compared to employees not being informed of the policy?

  - Would a public institution's network be considered a public
    network, and thus any traffic traveling over it being consider
    public also?

   - Any inclusion of electronic privacy, search and seizure, and the
    like in the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms pertains only
    to the protection of an individual's rights with respect to the 
    government or agents of the state (ie. police,courts).  I 
    expect this is different from an individual who is not associated
    with the state performing some act, such as network monitoring.

  - Does the creation and transmission of an electronic message, say
    email constitute publication?  Is it copywrited?  The reasoning
    behind this question follows.  Because of the ability to forge
    electronic mail, would it fall under the Criminal Code, Chapter
    C-46, Section 181 (Nuisances) "Every one who wilfully publishes
    a statement, tale or news..."  if the recipient received a 
    electronic communication, email, which was apparently from
    another, possibly real, person stating false information?
    Would C-46 S 430(1.1) (Mischief in relationg to Data) apply to
    the above example?

  - Does Chap C-46, Section 184(1) "... willfully intercepts a private 
    communication..." apply to computer based networks where the
    communication protocols pass every message to every other 
    computer on the network?

  - I believe that C-46 s345 "Stopping Mail with Intent" does not
    protect electronic communications, but only Canada Post mail.
    Would there be an equivalent protection for electronic mail?

  - Does 342.1 "Unauthorized use of Computer" hybrid apply to 
    the example of simple monitoring of network communications?
    Does this relate only to services, such as MCImail, or would
    it also include access to another individuals account? 
    (ie capturing usernames and passwords)

Thank you for your time and effort in assisting me to better 
understand the types of information I am dealing with.  I eagerly
await your response.

-- 
Blaine Bachelu				bachelu@cs.uregina.ca
Graduate Student
Department of Computer Science
University of Regina

