I think "game" vs "nongame" is not the best terminology. I think
"recreational" vs. "academic" is better, but we must remember that
there is no bright line dividing these categories. Some academics
study games and recreation and images of nudes. Also, the university,
at least ideally, encourages independent study, letting members of the
academic community decide for themselves what is academic.

Another thing to keep in mind is there is no such thing an inherently
harassing picture. For example, while _Playboy_ centerfolds at the
loading dock of the Physical Plant building might harass a female
worker there, _Playboy_ centerfolds in magazines in the library harass
no one. In other words, it is a situtation that is harassing, not a
thing.

Consider two scenarios.
   1) As research for a paper for the Computers, Freeedom, and Privacy
      conference, I display pictures on a workstation in a University
      coputer lab. Someone in the lab sees the displays and believes
      that I am sexually harassment him or her.

   2) For my own amusement, I display pictures on a workstation in a
      University computer lab (at a time when recreational use of the
      workstations is allowed). Someone in the lab sees the displays
      and believes that I am sexually harassment him or her.

I believe that if scenario 1 is SH, then so is scenario 2.  If
scenerio 2 is not SH, then neither is scenario 1. In other words, I
don't see that my reason for displaying the pictures matters.

If the displays are harassing, then I will have to stop and do
something like 1) display the images on my PC's (smaller) screen 2)
move to a workstation in the corner where no one can see it 3) work
in the lab when no one else is there.

If the displays are not harssing (and do not violate any other
university rules), the university has not basis for making me stop.

