In article <21ckq7$sm9@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>I fear I was unclear. I don't think the University (or any authority)
>should decide what is "offensive" and what is "inoffensive".

	But doesn't the cited policy in fact gives that University that
power, in the person of its officials? Who is the "reasonable person"
that makes the determination? I assume from your posting it is
"Assistant Dean for Sexual Harassment, Mary Ellen O'Shaughnessey".
	Can the policy *in effect* read:

	Sexual harassment is any . . . statement that Mary Ellen
	O'Shaughnessey finds offensive ...

	See the problem? I agree with the earlier point that a due
process determination is vital to a determination of sexual harassment.
Unfortunately, in a University environment, that often reduces to the
sole opinion of one (possibly very ideological) dean. Even if these
accusations must go to a disciplinary committee, the relevant dean, who
can define his or her job as prosecuting these cases, will be much more
experienced at presenting the prosecution case (and have an office of
resources to devote to it). The accused has probably never even heard of
case law. If he or she is so lucky as to be able to get meaningful
representation, a lawyer may not be allowed into the hearing. The
situation can be badly stacked against the accused.
	I believe you severely underestimate the "chilling effect". The
way it works in practice at MIT is that if there is a complaint about an
image, the person it told to remove it immediately or they may be
committing sexual harassment and will be sent to a dean. I've never
heard of anyone contesting such a "request". This is not because they
have voluntarily decided to accede when the matter is brought to their
attention, but because the expense of fighting the issue will be
tremendous. Lest someone reply "Who cares about a bunch of misogynists
getting off on nudie pics", note this has been applied far and wide.
	A critical factor is that these policies never define a class
of acceptable images or conduct. There is never an example that says
"such and such may be offensive to *you*, but it is not harassment.". So
it leaves the accused in the strange state of receiving a notice of the form
"You MAY have committed harassment. Contact the authorities to find out
your punishment if so.".

> 3) Is the recommended computer policy broader that the general university
>     definition or the legal definition?

	This is a complicated question. There's currently a Supreme
Court case that would clarify some of the law (what constitutes a
"hostile environment"). I think in terms of this discussion, the best
answer is something akin to "It is possible that if narrowly drawn and
ideally enforced, there would not be a major problem. But the
*combination* of the vague and broad terms of the definition, and the
*very weak* due process to be found in practice, will result in
substantial restrictions of freedom of expression.".

