machinations such a conclusion is made.  If the offending material is
for bona fide academic work, and was displayed as such without intent
to offend, the scholar must be protected from the charges.  A concrete
impairment of academic work is worse than any subjective impairment
of the complaintant's work caused by the offending material.

]>.  Suppose a person of a certain ethnic persuasion was offended by
]>historical research (including display of paraphenalia) on a group
]>bent on genocide?  Where will it end?

](Aside: I don't think the U of Illinois has a policy against ethnic
]harassment, but suppose it did). 

]Unless the offended person was some how compelled to view the display,
]there would be no harassment.

So, a stricter standard is to be applied only to harrassment of a sexual
nature?  The proposed guidelines state that incidental viewing of offensive
material is just cause for a complaint.  No one is ever compelled to view
my monitor.  One can easily avert the eyes, as you seem to imply in cases
of hypothetical ethnic harrassment.


In your followup, you didn't address my claim that the proposed guidelines
don't call for cessation of the offending behavior.  The claim is that only
the Office of (the Inquisition) can make a determination of whether sexual
harrassment has occurred; that sysadmins can't make that call.

I oppose that policy for these reasons:

1) It is quite possible that a sysadmin informing the user that a person in
  the lab is offended will be quite enough to cease the offending viewing.
  The sysadmin does not make a determination as to whether the image is
  actually harrassing or not.  If a belligerent user insists that the image
  is non-harrassing and thus allowed, then the case can be decided formally.

2) The policy of a formal hearing with any complaint has a "chilling effect"
  because the policy explicitly refuses to make a decision based upon the
  content, instead basing it on the impressions of the voyeuristic victim,
  and some non-defined "resonable person" standard.  

3) It further has a "chilling effect" because this is an after-the-fact
  determination of guilt or innocence, with no definition of the "crime."
  It reminds me of the (para)quote: I can't expressly define obscenity,
  "but I know it when I see it."  One can't know whether they're violating
  University policy until he or she is before a board, subject to career-
  threatening discipline.  

4) This is "big stick" regulation.  It is intimidation.  People will avoid
  probably-innocent actions out of fear.  It is worse than explict 
  prohibition of certain materials.

I'm getting redundant, so I'll stop now.

