Absolutly!

The University must explain its policy so that everyone can understand it.  Sorry,
though, 'standard practice' is unhealthy belief.  Most 'standard practices' are
not written down nor commonly explained, so a 'standard practice' isn't a safe
designation.

My point is simple.  All policies should be clearly explained to the users,
'standard practice' or not.  If the policy is well understood, but for some
reason, access is still avalible, the user is WRONG for accessing the information.
If the policy is not clearly understood, that is the system adminstrations fault.

If, however, the user is doing something they know is wrong, policy of no, and they
still do it, this is wrong.  Here is the crux.  The user has the responsibility to
'not break the system' as the system adminstration has to 'make the system not
breakable.'  It isn't as one sided as most of the people in this area obvously
think.  Its both sides.  A user can't access information they know they shouldn't,
and the system administartion has to clearly define the policies.

This is where this particular case breaks down on the side of the user (at least
from the info we have been given):

  1) The policy of only accessing 'your' data isn't understood.
  2) The user had what they felt was a justifiable reason for this data AND knew
     that the security risk was minor AND it wasn't against written policy.
  3) By punishing the user instead of instructing the user, the University
     failed.  Even if the system was 'at risk' in their opinion, they should have
     explained, and WATCHED (without breaking privacy rules).  If the user still
     continued to break this policy, then you have a reason for punishment.

First rule of human relations.  Don't assume.  Communicate.  Whenever someone
says, 'standard practice' or 'always', your setting yourself up for trouble.
Communicate the policies and ideas clearly ON BOTH SIDES.

I happen to agree, practice and consult the 'standard practices.'  But, I also
tell the people I do work for to communicate the policies to the users clearly.

Chris


