>In this case, the person doesn't claim ignorance and the difference is one of
>policy.  According to what they told him, the 'acceptable use' is to access ONLY
>files that are 'his.'  If this policy is not written, explained or burned into
>the computer screen, then it is not reasonable.  Curiosity is natural.  It is not
>presumed that you can't access 'anything outside of your files.'

Once again we have a quibble over whether or not a file belongs to someone.
It has been clearly shown that the information in /etc/passwd as well as many
other system files can be gotten through indirect means.  And for those who
really like to walk the line, through semi-direct means, like copying, cat-ing
the file into a buffer, etc...

And if it looks like a user list, smells like a user list, and has no 
passwords (encrypted or not) then it is probably used as a user list.

>Now, in this case, the user did consider the implications, decided that there was 
>a legit reason to access the file and that there was no security violation.  As 
