Because then you'll have to be very specific in your definition of access.  
Will you mean you cannot - more, cat, vi, emacs, cp, ftp, dcc - a file?  
What about all the indirect access that occurse with commands 
like - ls, finger ?

You simply cannot put restrictions on world readable files without getting
into a giant semantic mess.  This is the reason that world readable system 
files on most decently secures unix machines have information that does not 
allow a user to directly crack the system.  Encrypted passwords may be a 
touchy subject but they are being phased out, and to get them one usually 
has an account on the machine.  And if one has an account on a machine the 
ways of getting root are just to numerous to call "looking" at the 
/etc/passwd file a crime.  If he was trying to break into the system that's 
another thing.  But with the file he had, that would have been very 
difficult.
 
