	The problem is with exactly what constitutes a 'hostile environment'.
The _Meritor v. Vinson_ case is usually cited as approximately
"establishing that a hostile environment is grounds for legal action
under Title VII".  But the explanation usually doesn't go further. If you
actually read the case, you will find that the 'environment' cited was:

"According to respondent, Taylor thereafter made repeated demands upon
her for sexual favors, usually at the branch, both during and after
business hours; she estimated that over the next several years she had
intercourse with him some 40 or 50 times.  In addition, respondent
testified that Taylor fondled her in front of other employees, followed
her into the women's restroom when she went there alone, exposed himself
to her, and even forcibly raped her on several occasions."

	The phrase 'hostile environment' has now been extended beyond
all resemblance to the holding of _Meritor_. It is typically invoked
against any image (or anything at all), under theories similar to those
propounded by the well-known Dworkin and MacKinnon. The case law is in
flux, which is why the Supreme Court currently has the issue before it.
In a recent _legal_ (hence public) case involving a (male) MIT professor
accused of sexual harassment by a (female) employee, the differences
became very clear. The defense argued for a standard where the operative
part was "severe and pervasive", the plantiff's attorney argued "even if
an *unreasonably* sensitive person finds ..." (emphasis mine) (this from
the local campus newspaper; I dearly hope it was a misprint). The judge
(a woman, incidently) instructed the jury to use the standard with
"severe and pervasive", and the professor was acquited. Note the case is
being appealed.
	Dworkin-MacKinnon-like theory is not the current legal standard,
though it may become so in the future :-(. But because accused students
have such poor due-process protection, can be "tried" before *very*
biased deans, and are greatly outmatched in experience, time and
resources, they typically have little hope of defending expression
aginst an official who has decided to enforce regulations under a
Dworkin-Mackinnon (i.e. extreme censorship) standard.

--
