We take a different tack. We allow *any* legal activity on our computers
(legal meaning not violating federal or state law, university, usenet,
nsfnet, or nearnet policy, and a few "good neighbor" add-ons) with a
resource based restriction; low priority stuff must happen only during
non-busy times. It's fairly easy to enforce the idea of prime time
because it makes sense (all of our users have had to suffer when the
system is busy, so they *know*)
  We've found that games, including the search for them and the
installation of them, can be a great teaching tool. Your average
undergraduate lab assistant will willingly spend HOURS mastering the
intimate tricks of X-windows installation to install the latest xtank
game. You then have a student X installation wizard who can install
any other X software, for example. For beginners, Nethack can be a
great way to master the vi editing tools.
   I'm serious here. We enforce no games or gif transfers during busy
times, but I don't see the point in banning anything for its own sake.
By definition a non-busy time is a time when there are spare
resources.

Betsy

