drafting this (??).  

As I mentioned before, my impression is that though the courts (unfortunately,
IMO) *have* been willing to uphold restrictive regulations on speech in the
working environment (as evidenced by the Jacksonville Shipyards case), they have
*NOT* been willing to do the same in the university environment (as evidenced by
the UWM post case).  However, I'm not a lawyer and sometimes the weird things
the courts decide really baffle me.  Presumably the university can get some
genuine 100% official paid-for legal advice on this matter, and I hope that they
do.

More importantly (perhaps) than what the courts might decide, I think that the
proposed regulations, besides being likely to be shot down by the courts, are
simply wrong-headed.

I think that in the interests of academic freedom, universities *especially*
should make an effort to tolerate different points of view, and not to regulate
'offensive' speech merely because it is offensive.

The safest standard (from a legal point of view) is to insist that only displays
which tend to provoke immediate disorderly behavior should be regulated.  I
personally could see extending this a bit to a somewhat less extreme standard
(though I'm not sure what the courts reaction would be) - something that made it
'unreasonably difficult' to study or work (such as very loud noises, perhaps)
emenating from a person or computer would be a resonable candidate (IMO) for
university regulation.

However, I am concerned by the apparent inability of university administrators
to distinguish between reasonable and unreasonable behavior.  For instance, at
one university, a professor was ordered to take down a picture of his *wife* (in
a bikini) that he had on his desk.  In another university, Goya nudes were taken
down after 'sexual harssment' complaints.  IMO, this is simply silly, but
this seems to be where university speech regulations are headed nowadays.

