In article <mumble>, karl@genesis.MCS.COM (Karl Denninger) writes:
|> The point here is to enable you to vote with your wallet and feet, a concept
|> foreign to most educational institutions.  This would automatically put
|> abuses such as UH in immediate check -- they would then risk losing their
|> students, and thus enough of their funding base that they would collapse of
|> their own weight.

Doubtful.  Look at the financial reports of any major university,
public or private.  The contribution to overall income from tuition is
minor at many private universities and downright trivial at many
(most?) state funded schools, including UH.  At Rice, which has only 4000
students, tuition makes up about 25% of operating income each year.
That is probably on the high side for private universities.
The only places where it is most likely that tuition is a majority
factor in the income of the institution are very small private
colleges or universities without significant endowments.  While there
may be quite a few of these institutions, they only educate a small
percentage of all those who attend universities.  State schools get the
bulk of students, and tuition at them for state residents comes
nowhere near covering the actual cost of the education provided.  Most
state schools have financial difficulties because the state
legislature does not vote enough funding to run them, not because
tuition income goes down.

|> 
|> Those undertaking a post-graduate study level should insist on a contractual
|> document before signing up and matriculating for said course of study.  By
|> then I would expect that students would know enough of the world to both
|> insist on and hold colleges accountable to those contracts.

Graduate students routinely get tuition waivers, fellowships or
scholarships.  Most graduate students are studying on OPM - other
people's money.  Thus, while a form of contractual arrangement exists
between the graduate student and the university, the concept of "I paid
my money, so you owe me a degree" doesn't hold true a lot of the time -
it wasn't their money to start with.  In addition, the very nature of
graduate study - a large component of directed, original research -
means that the determination that they have acquired the requisite
knowledge to be awarded a Masters or Doctorate is necessarily
subjective.  Baccalaureate degrees are somewhat different, because the
bulk of the basis for the certification is a demonstration that you have
acquired a certain amount of relatively well-defined knowledge about a
subject.

I would like to address some follow on statements you made in a
slightly later post:

> a)	Universities are held to provide professional services to an
> 	untrained body of people.  Thus, they have a professional level 
> 	of duty in disclosure and performance. (see the UCC for a more wordy
> 	version of this standard of performance)  In fact, I think a case

Untrained body of people?  Universities provide a structured process
for you to obtain specific sets of knowledge, and will certify that you
have demonstrated that you have obtained that knowledge.  You don't
have to go to a university to get this knowledge, either.  Ultimately
the actual obtaining of the knowledge can only be done by the student.
Quite the contrary, I would assert that people are uniquely and
innately capable of actually obtaining the knowledge.  Nothing that a
university does will actually put the knowledge in your head.  Only you
can do that.  The university can only make it easier for you to put a
specific set of knowledge into your brain.  Additionally since the
university's process is quite similar to the process used by primary
education systems, university students are even trained in the
structured process itself when they arrive.

You are attempting to apply a standard that is applied to say,
lawyers.  But that is different.  A lawyer makes use of knowledge and
training that I don't have, but could acquire if I wanted to, to
perform a function that *I* am required to or desire to do.  I can
write my own will and my own contracts, and argue my cases before the
courts.  It may be amazingly contrary to my interests to do so because
I am so ignorant or inexperienced on the subject, but I can do these
things without a lawyer.  If I took the time to learn the law, I might
even do these things competently.  Using a lawyer simply shortens the
process of doing these things, and vastly improves the probability that
the affair will be handled in my best interest.  I make use of his/her
knowledge and experience in lieu of spending the time to acquire it
myself.  The same is true of accountants, engineers, doctors,
pharmacists and a large number of other professions.  The standards of
disclosure and performance are there to assure me that the person is
acting in my best interest, since it is unlikely that I can judge that
they are doing so without having the knowledge that they do.  On the
other hand, students are not making use of a university's knowledge in
lieu of acquiring it themselves.  They are in fact acquiring it
themselves!


> b)	From (a), we come up with a liability formula which basically
> 	states:
> 	[...]
> 	3)	If the school violates this standard then you are entitled
> 		to a full refund of all monies paid to date, PLUS monetary
> 		damages amounting to $X for each month which you spent
> 		attempting to earn your degree (which you've now been
> 		denied/delayed).  (Ie: if you're "suspended" for a year in
> 		violation of this, you're owed $12X).  I would also impose
> 		punitive damages of $10,000 or one year's tuition and fees
> 		per occurrance as an incentive to colleges to not do this
> 		kind of thing.

There are undoubtedly students paying full price for their educations,
but they are generally a minority on campuses, and even then "full
price", i.e. full tuition, covers only a minor portion of the actual
cost of educating that student.  Given this, a full refund of tuition
if you are kicked out is not necessarily appropriate.  Particularly at
a state school, the cost of educating that student has equalled or
exceeded his or her tuition payment after a short time.  At a major
state school, I would imagine that a student is into a negative
balance after only a few weeks, meaning that the cost of having that
student attend classes and possibly live at that university to that
point exceeds the tuition they paid.  If that student breaks the rules
late in the year, it is in fact *unreasonable* that the university
should give the student money back -  the university has already spent
far more on that student that they contributed to the university.
Punitive damages to the university would only exacerbate the imbalance.

A final point about your liability standard involves difficulty with
the nature of commercial transactions, and understood by the UCC.  A
commercial transaction involves exchange of consideration (money,
usually) for a product or service.  Either party can conditionally
cancel the transaction by reversing the exchange.  Give the product
back and get your money back.  No service rendered, no payment
required.  Give the money back, expect the product back.   But what is
the "product" of a university?  It is not the piece of paper you pick
up at a graduation ceremony.  That is merely a psychological gesture
for the sake of your ego.  The "product" of a university is knowledge.
And once given, it cannot be taken back.  If you leave the university,
you don't instantly forget everything you learned, and there is no way
for the university to make this happen.  Thus, your consideration
(money) was given in exchange for knowledge.  If you don't give the
knowledge back, the University is not obligated to give the money
back.

> 
> c)	The standard of care due to students is a professional one (ie: you
> 	can also sue for professional malpractice or malfeasance)

Malpractice and malfeasance are generally applied to persons who make
use of specialized knowledge that you don't have and act on your behalf
(sort out your finances, do your taxes, take care of your legal
matters, become involved in your health care, determine that your
building won't fall down under normal circumstances, etc.)  It's quite
a stretch for me to see how this applies to universities facilitating
students' acquisition of knowledge.

> 
> d)	Your academic record is your property.  Period.

If this is based on the "who pays, owns," property principle, then by
and large the academic record is in fact *University* property, since
the overwhelming cost of producing the information in that record was
paid by the University.  This is unlike a medical record, where by and
large, the patient (or the patient's insurer) in fact paid the cost of
producing the information contained in that record.

