>     As the owner/editor/coordinator of several LISTSERV lists,
> including Russia and Ukraine, I am writing tonight with a heavy
> heart as a result of Microsoft's dismissal of Gregory Nicholas
> Steshenko, citing objections to his participation on these lists.
> 
>     As subscribers of both lists know only too well, Gregg and I
> have frequently been at odds regarding the appropriateness of
> some of his postings to these lists.  Gregg is one of three list
> subscribers about whom I have received voluminous private traffic,
> most of it requesting his removal from the list.  A few weeks ago,
> during a particularly bad stretch of flaming on list Ukraine, I
> placed the list in moderated mode to try to regain our collective
> composure and focus.  When the advent of list moderation generated
> even more dissension, I conducted a straw poll of all list
> subscribers asking what format (open and unmoderated, open but
> moderated, or closed) was preferred;  the overwhelming response
> was for open and unmoderated.  I have abided by that collective
> decision, and will continue to honor it unless or until another
> poll garners different results.
> 
>     Throughout the recent controversy on list Ukraine (which has
> often spilled over on to the Russia list), I have made no effort
> to remove *any* subscriber from either list.  Nor have I voiced
> any concern regarding subscriber behavior on the list to anyone
> other than the subscriber her- or himself (or in an occasional
> fit of pique, the list itself).  Whatever my objections or concerns
> regarding the behavior of a particular subscriber on the list, I
> have a fundamental and abiding commitment to freedom of expression.
> The most difficult part of coordinating the Russia and Ukraine
> lists is the balancing act between the right of the individual to
> express her or his own opinion, and the right of list subscribers
> collectively to be treated with common human dignity and respect
> in the process of conducting their online discussions.
>  
>     I am simultaneously angered, frightened and saddened by Microsoft's
> apparent dismissal of Gregg Steshenko citing his controversial
> participation on these lists.  I've spoken with Gregg this evening by
> phone, and have learned that apparently earlier this week Microsoft
> asked him not to post to the lists from his Microsoft userid.  This
> seems to me to be not an unreasonable request by an employer;  the
> employer effectively owns the tools the employee uses in the
> performance of his or her duties, and it doesn't seem unreasonable for
> the employer to exercise some degree of control over those tools.
